## Rules

- Make your own clean clone: `git clone -q --no-local SOURCE READERDIR/repo`
  then `git -C READERDIR/repo checkout -q 9b69ca5f`, where SOURCE and READERDIR
  are given in your prompt. Work only there. **Every file you write, including
  files a tool derives from an input (outputs next to inputs, caches, venvs),
  goes under READERDIR.** Never write to SOURCE.
- Read-only toward everything else: no `git push`, no `gh` writes, no ssh, no
  paid model or API calls, no network beyond what `uv sync` needs inside your
  clone. Never read or source any `.env` file.
- You may run tests in your clone (`uv sync -q --group dev` then
  `uv run pytest -q ...`). Keep runs focused; the full lane is run elsewhere.
- Treat every claim as unproved until you reproduce it. A finding needs a
  concrete reproduction (a command and its output, or a quoted line with
  file:line in the candidate).
- Apply the repository's "code fitted to its examples" rule (AGENTS.md): read
  each new regular expression, string split, slice and special case as a claim
  that it covers its input class; build a legal input the tests lack, run it,
  and report a wrong result naming the input.
- In your report, write local paths as `<scratch>`; never print a home
  directory, user name or email.

## Report (your final message, Markdown, under 1,500 words)

1. Digest check (each of the four: matches or not).
2. Findings table: Id (R<n>-F<k>), file:line, what is wrong, reproduction,
   class (`blocking` = the close cannot honestly claim this; `hardening`;
   `completeness`), suggested fix.
3. What checked out (briefly, with what you ran).
4. Verification limits.
5. A last line `VERDICT: APPROVE`, `VERDICT: APPROVE_WITH_FIXES` or
   `VERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED`.
